\"width\":0,\"linetype\":[]},\"yref\":\"paper\",\"xref\":\"paper\",\"x0\":0,\"x1\":1,\"y0\":0,\"y1\":1}],\"showlegend\":false,\"legend\":{\"bgcolor\":null,\"bordercolor\":null,\"borderwidth\":0,\"font\":{\"color\":\"rgba(0,0,0,1)\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":11.68949771689498}},\"hovermode\":\"closest\",\"barmode\":\"relative\"},\"config\":{\"doubleClick\":\"reset\",\"modeBarButtonsToAdd\":[\"hoverclosest\",\"hovercompare\"],\"showSendToCloud\":false,\"displayModeBar\":false},\"source\":\"A\",\"attrs\":{\"382826ca47e1\":{\"x\":{},\"y\":{},\"text\":{},\"type\":\"scatter\"},\"38282c472bbf\":{\"y\":{}}},\"cur_data\":\"382826ca47e1\",\"visdat\":{\"382826ca47e1\":[\"function (y) \",\"x\"],\"38282c472bbf\":[\"function (y) \",\"x\"]},\"highlight\":{\"on\":\"plotly_click\",\"persistent\":false,\"dynamic\":false,\"selectize\":false,\"opacityDim\":0.20000000000000001,\"selected\":{\"opacity\":1},\"debounce\":0},\"shinyEvents\":[\"plotly_hover\",\"plotly_click\",\"plotly_selected\",\"plotly_relayout\",\"plotly_brushed\",\"plotly_brushing\",\"plotly_clickannotation\",\"plotly_doubleclick\",\"plotly_deselect\",\"plotly_afterplot\",\"plotly_sunburstclick\"],\"base_url\":\"https://plot.ly\"},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThis is the monthly principal and interest on the median listed home, with 10% down and a 30-year fixed mortgage at\nthat month's average rate. It doubled from 2021 to 2023 as rates shot up, eased in 2025 as rates fell, and has climbed\nagain this fall.\n\n## Are prices really falling?\n\nIt depends on which measure you use:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|Measure                                      |   Latest| Change from a year ago|\n|:--------------------------------------------|--------:|----------------------:|\n|Median listing price (Realtor.com)           | $415,000|                  -2.2%|\n|Median price of new homes sold (Census)      | $393,300|                  -4.1%|\n|Case-Shiller home price index (repeat sales) |    336.7|                  +2.1%|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n**Asking prices** and **new-home prices** are down. But the **Case-Shiller index**, which tracks the same homes selling again\nover time and is often the best measure of true price changes, is still slightly **up** over the year, just off its summer\npeak. So \"prices are dipping\" is fair; \"prices are falling fast\" is not.\n\n## The buyer's market signals\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|Signal                            |    Latest| Change from a year ago|\n|:---------------------------------|---------:|----------------------:|\n|Homes listed for sale             | 1,183,520|                  +7.4%|\n|Existing homes sold (yearly pace) | 3,750,000|                  -8.8%|\n|Median days on market             |        63|                   0.0%|\n|Rents (CPI)                       |          |                  +3.0%|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nMore homes for sale and fewer sales means sellers are competing for buyers, not the other way around. That can mean\nroom to negotiate on price, repairs, or seller-paid closing costs. Meanwhile rents are still rising, so waiting isn't free\neither.\n\n## Things to weigh\n\nThe data can't tell you whether *you* should buy, but it does point to the questions that matter most:\n\n- **Can you afford the payment at today's rate?** Some people buy hoping to refinance later if rates drop. That can\n  work, but rates aren't guaranteed to fall, and refinancing has costs.\n- **How long will you stay?** Buying and selling costs thousands; it usually takes several years for owning to beat\n  renting.\n- **Use the leverage.** In a slower market, asking for a lower price or concessions is more realistic than it was in 2021–2022.\n\n## The fine print\n\n- **Payments shown are principal and interest only.** Property taxes, insurance, and HOA fees can add hundreds a month.\n- **National medians.** Local markets vary widely; some areas are seeing real price drops, others are still rising.\n- **The median listing price** reflects the mix of homes for sale, not just price changes.\n\n## The bottom line\n\nHome prices are softening, and buyers have more choices and leverage than they've had in years. But mortgage rates\nclimbed even more than prices dipped, so the typical monthly payment is higher than a year ago. Whether that makes\nnow a good time depends far more on your budget and timeline than on the headlines.\n\n---\n\n**How we did this.** Median listing price, active listings, and days on market are from Realtor.com; new-home prices\nfrom the Census Bureau; the Case-Shiller U.S. National Home Price Index from S&P; existing home sales from the National\nAssociation of Realtors; mortgage rates from Freddie Mac's weekly survey (averaged by month); and rents from the BLS CPI,\nall via [FRED](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/). Payments assume 10% down and a 30-year fixed loan.\n",
